    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, i am studying phonetics and im a bit confused. can u explain the difference between voiced and unvoiced fricatives? like why do some sounds like /s/ sound different from /z/ even though my mouth is in the same place?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Fricatives, like /s/ and /z/, are all about forcing air through a narrow gap in your vocal tract, creating a hissing sound. The difference between voiced and unvoiced, however, comes down to the vibration of your vocal cords. When you produce an unvoiced fricative, like /s/, your vocal cords are held open and do not vibrate. In contrast, for a voiced fricative, like /z/, your vocal cords buzz or vibrate as the air passes through. This vibration is what gives /z/ its fuller, buzzing quality compared to the sharp, airy sound of /s/.",
      "source": "target",
